#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Fan-in entry point for this repo's dotfiles:* mise tasks"
#MISE depends=["dotfiles:completions"]
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PoC #747 C7. Proves `depends` can stand in for the numeric run_after_NN filename ordering chezmoi
# scripts use today: a caller (or a future chezmoi wrapper script) invokes this one task instead of
# having to know and order every dotfiles:* task by name. No body of its own -- `depends` above is
# the whole task. As later phases add tasks, they get added to that list; nothing below this comment
# block should ever need to change to accommodate them.
#
# Only one dependency exists today. The other two vehicles §C's fan-in was originally written
# against are deliberately not in this list:
#   - dotfiles:krew stays a script (#747 C3 verdict -- see poc/README.md): sources/outputs makes its
#     staleness cache lie on a deleted receipt or a mid-run failure, so it was never converted to a
#     task in the first place. Nothing to depend on here.
#   - dotfiles:packages was deliberately not built. Its two reasons for existing in the ticket are
#     both already resolved elsewhere: bootstrap packages are wired into
#     run_after_20_mise-standard.sh.tmpl's apply_bootstrap_packages helper (§B), and the
#     run_onchange_ + task question it existed to provoke has its own dedicated, already-answered
#     probe (#747 C5). Building it now would ship a mechanism the evidence says not to use just to
#     have a third fan-in member.
#
# What a one-member fan-in does and does not show: it proves `depends` resolves and runs cleanly,
# and that a cached (skipped-as-up-to-date) dependency doesn't break the aggregate -- the property
# that makes a single entry point viable as later phases add tasks. It does NOT prove the mechanism
# scales past one member: ordering between multiple dependencies, partial failure inside the set,
# and fan-out width are all unexercised here and remain open questions for phase 5.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
